  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「セイヨウノコギリソウ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%BB%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A8%E3%82%A6%E3%83%8E%E3%82%B3%E3%82%AE%E3%83%AA%E3%82%BD%E3%82%A6",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 107381605,
      "text": "セイヨウノコギリソウ は、ヨーロッパ原産のキク科ノコギリソウ属に分類される多年草の1種。英名はヤロウ (yarrow)。牧草学ではヤローとよび、ヒツジに好まれるとされる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Achillea millefolium」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achillea_millefolium",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1377049458,
      "text": "Achillea millefolium, commonly known as yarrow or common yarrow, is a flowering plant in the family Asteraceae. Growing to 1 metre tall, it is characterized by small whitish flowers, a tall stem of fernlike leaves, and a pungent odor."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Achillea millefolium」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achillea_millefolium#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1377049458,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Achillea millefolium is cultivated as an ornamental plant by many plant nurseries. It is planted in gardens and natural landscaping settings of diverse climates and styles. They include native plant, drought-tolerant, and wildlife gardens. The plant is a frequent component of butterfly gardens. The plant prefers well-drained soil in full sun, but can be grown in less ideal conditions. Field trials in Kansas found yarrow produced an average yield of 110,000 plants per hectare (43,000/acre), with a total dry weight of 11,800 kg/ha (10,500 pounds per acre).\n\n■ Propagation\nFor propagation, seeds require light for germination, so optimal germination occurs when planted no deeper than 6 mm (1⁄4 in). Seeds also require a germination temperature of 18–24 °C (64–75 °F). It has a relatively short life in some situations, but may be prolonged by division in the spring every other year, and planting 30 to 46 cm (12–18 in) apart. It can become invasive.\n\n■ Cultivars\n\nThe species use in traditional gardens has generally been superseded by cultivars with specific 'improved' qualities. Some are used as drought-tolerant lawns, with periodic mowing. The many different ornamental cultivars include: 'Paprika', 'Cerise Queen', 'Red Beauty', 'Red Velvet', 'Saucy Seduction', 'Strawberry Seduction' (red), 'Island Pink' (pink), 'Calistoga' (white), and 'Sonoma Coast' (white). The following are recipients of the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit:\n\n'Credo'\n'Lachsschönheit' (Galaxy Series)\n'Martina'\n'Lansdorferglut'\nThe many hybrids of this species designated Achillea × taygetea are useful garden subjects, including: 'Appleblossom', 'Fanal', 'Hoffnung', and 'Moonshine'.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    },
    "toxicity": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Achillea millefolium」の節「Toxicity」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achillea_millefolium#Toxicity",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1377049458,
        "heading": "Toxicity",
        "text": "Yarrow can cause allergic skin rashes.\nAccording to the American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals, yarrow is toxic to dogs, cats, and horses, causing increased urination, vomiting, diarrhea and dermatitis. When consumed by cows, an unfavorable flavor is given to their milk.\nAdditionally, due to its superficial similarity to the highly toxic hemlock, the confusion of the two species can be deadly.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
